  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nIR currently shows an upper level low over Nevada with cooling\ncloud tops pushing into northern Utah and far southwestern\nreaches of Wyoming. This will be the ignition for thunderstorm\ndevelopment come later Tuesday afternoon and evening as it\npushes northeast through the morning hours. WV shows the main\nfinger of the PFJ extending around a low over the Gulf of Alaska\nextending into Canada, with a weak finger of 60-70kts around the\naforementioned low over Nevada. With not much support, it is\nforecasted to become more of an open wave as it moves over the\nCWA in the afternoon and evening. With mostly clear skies for\nmuch of the CWA, coupled with recent rainfall, fog development\nis possible in eastern Natrona County around Casper by sunrise\nTuesday morning, but confidence low with small hint of upslope\neasterly flow by the FV3 sticking with 10-20% chance.\nRegardless, this will burn off quickly with a slightly warmer\nafternoon in store than it was Monday by 2 to 5 degrees. \n\nWith the approaching system from the southwest, expect scattered\nrain shower activity with a few isolated thunderstorms to push\nin from northeastern Utah west of the Divide by 11AM to noon\nwith northward progression through the evening hours. This will\nextend across the Absarokas and Bighorn Basin by late afternoon\nexiting into Montana by 7-8PM weakening with radiational cooling\ntowards sunset. The main concern will be with a stronger push of\nPVA anomaly into western Sweetwater County by 4-5PM extending\nover the southern Winds into the Wind River Basin by 6-7PM\nbefore exiting across Natrona/Johnson Counties to the northeast\nby 9-10PM. The main threats will be gusty outflow winds up to 50\nto 55mph. MLCAPE values are forecasted to increase to 6-700 J/kg\nby late afternoon with forecast sounding output of shear values\nover 30kts that will help aid in the stronger winds. Not quite\nsevere is expected, with much of that activity into northern\nUtah to Uinta County due to stronger aid of peak heating of the\nafternoon earlier than when it pushes into the RIW CWA. Heavy\ndownpours will not be as high of concern being a bit more\nprogressive than over the weekend with the low becoming more\nopen with its northeastward progression into the upper Great\nPlains by midnight Tuesday night. It fills with time as well\nwith decreased divergence aloft and ridging builds in from the\nsouth. \n\nWith that said, a warming trend and dry conditions expected for\nthe rest of the work week into much of the weekend. Above\naverage temperatures in the afternoon by Thursday extending\nthrough Saturday ahead of the next system that could affect the\nupper Rockies by late Sunday from the previously mentioned GOA\nlow. Longer term model solutions aren't in total agreement on\nits progression to the Pacific northwest and later through the\nRockies but something to watch in the coming days for late\nSunday into early next week. For now, enjoy the rain chances for\nTuesday, as it will be a few days at least until the next decent\nchance.",
